------------------------------------------- WebSockets ------------------------------------------------


- GET REAL TIME DATA FROM THE SERVER OVER WEBSOCKETS!

- Time to do some real-time communication!
    🧠WebSockets are a (relatively) recent protocol to do bi-directional real-time communication with
    your server: it will open a TCP connection with your server and both the browser and the server
    can send data to each other.

- ⚠️An error can sometimes happen because of a proxy or a firewall in your network infrastructure that
    would not let WebSocket communications go through. If it is the case for you, try with another
    internet connection without proxy or firewall (for example from a cellphone connection): it should
    then be OK.

- Instruction:

1️⃣ 🔥RxJS is well suited to WebSockets: you can create an Observable from a WebSocket connection directly
    with the webSocket operator!
    🔥We are also going to use a higher level protocol called Stomp over WebSockets, with a library
    called webstomp-client. 🔥This allows to do broadcasting, in a pub/sub fashion, over a classic
    WebSocket connection (really handy on the server side).

    Add this library to your application:

        npm install --save-exact webstomp-client@1.2.6
    
    To use this library, we also need to make the TypeScript compiler happy.
    For many libraries, this is pretty easy, as their typings are already included in the NPM package
    or available in the DefinitelyTyped repository, and we can install them using NPM with
    
        npm install --save-dev --save-exact @types/my-library
    
    (all the typings from DefinitelyTyped are available as a NPM package under the @types scoped package).
    🧠 after installing webstomp-client, if you see inside the folder

        node_modules
        |- webstomp-client
            |- package.json
            |- index.d.ts
        
    (inside package.json)
        {
            "types": "index.d.ts",
        }

    it reads the package's package.json, finds the "types" entry, loads index.d.ts, and now it knows:
    -> what functions exist,
    -> what parameters they accept,
    -> what they return.
    ✅No additional installation is necessary. Therefore we don't actually need to run second command.
    For Webstomp, the typings are also included by default in the NPM package, so nothing to do!
    😜Let’s now use this library!



2️⃣ To encapsulate the WebSockets logic, create a new service ws with Angular CLI.

    🔥This service should have a connect method, subscribing to a Stomp channel, and returning an
    Observable<T>. 🔥🔥Indeed an Observable is a really good representation of a Stomp subscription:
    messages or errors can happen at any time, and the stream can complete.

    🔥We are going to wrap our WebSocket connection in an Observable. To do so the connect method
    should return a new Observable right away created with:

        connect<T>(channel): Observable<T> {
            return new Observable((observer: Observer<T>) => {
                // TODO create the WebSocket connection
                // TODO create the stomp client with Webstomp
                // TODO connect the stomp client
                // TODO subscribe to the specific channel and store the subscription
                // TODO handle incoming messages
                // TODO handle the unsubscription
            });
        }
    
    🧠✅Note that the method is generically typed with <T>, allowing us to call it like
    ws.connect<UserModel>() to let TypeScript know that the Observable returned emits events of type
    UserModel.

    🧠new Observable((observer: Observer<T>) => {}) creates an observable. The function passed as a
    parameter to the constructor is called the subscribe function: it will be responsible for emitting
    events and errors, and to complete when done.
    
    🧠For example, if you want to create an Observable which emits 1, then 2, then completes, you could do:

        const numbers = new Observable(observer => {
            observer.next(1);
            observer.next(2);
            observer.complete();
        });
    
    🧠Now we could subscribe to such an observable:

        numbers.subscribe({
            next: number => console.log(number),
            error: error => console.log(error),
            complete: () => console.log('Complete!')
        });
        // Will log:
        // 1
        // 2
        // Complete!
    
    🧠The function passed to new Observable() can also return a function that will be called on the
    unsubscription:

        const numbers = new Observable(observer => {
            observer.next(1);
            observer.next(2);
            observer.complete();
            return () => console.log('The subscriber decided to unsubscribe!') 👈
        });

        const subscription = numbers.subscribe(
            // we unsubscribe as soon as we receive a number
            number => subscription.unsubscribe()
        );
        // Will log:
        // The subscriber decided to unsubscribe!
    
    🔥That’s really useful if you have some cleanup to do (like us, because we’ll need to close the
    communication when the observable will be unsubscribed).



3️⃣ We now need to implement the logic of each TODO inside the subscribe function. 
    🧠🔥To create the connection, we’ll use WebSocket, an object provided by the browser. Create a new
    connection:

        const connection: WebSocket = new WebSocket(`${environment.wsBaseUrl}/ws`);
    
    This will attempt to establish a WebSocket connection with our backend. Create the wsBaseUrl
    attribute in the environment constant with the value: wss://ponyracer.ninja-squad.com.

    🧠🔥Now let’s use Webstomp to create the stomp client:

        const stompClient: WebstompClient.Client = WebstompClient.over(connection);
    
    🧠🔥Don’t forget the necessary import:

        import * as WebstompClient from 'webstomp-client';
    
    🧠🔥Webstomp gives us a client on which we can call connect(headers, connectionCallback). We
    don’t have specific headers to give, so we’ll give it { login: '', passcode: '' }:

        let subscription: WebstompClient.Subscription;
        stompClient.connect({ login: '', passcode: '' }, () => {
            // TODO subscribe to the specific channel
            // TODO emit the message received, after extracting the JSON from the body
        }, error => {
            // propagate the error
            observer.error(error);
        });
        // TODO handle the unsubscription
    
    🧠🔥To subscribe to a specific channel, there is a method called subscribe(channel, onMessageCallback):

        subscription = stompClient.subscribe(channel, message => {
            // TODO emit the message received, after extracting the JSON from the body
        });
    
    🧠🔥The final step is to handle the incoming messages in the callback, and send them into our
    Observable by using observer.next(message):

        const bodyAsJson = JSON.parse(message.body) as T;
        observer.next(bodyAsJson);
    
    🧠🔥And to return the function to call on an unsubscription, where we’ll close all our
    communications (Webstomp subscription and WebSocket connection):

        return () => {
            if (subscription) {
                subscription.unsubscribe();
            }
            connection.close();
        };
    
    ✅ Final connect method:

        connect<T>(channel: string): Observable<T> {
            return new Observable((observer: Subscriber<T>) => {
                // 👇create the WebSocket connection
                const connection: WebSocket = new WebSocket(`${environment.wsBaseUrl}/ws`);

                // 👇create the stomp client with Webstomp
                const stompClient: WebstompClient.Client = WebstompClient.over(connection);

                // 👇connect the stomp client
                let subscription: WebstompClient.Subscription;
                stompClient.connect({ login: '', passcode: '' }, () => {
                    // 👇subscribe to the specific channel
                    subscription = stompClient.subscribe(channel, message => {
                        // 👇emit the message received, after extracting the JSON from the body
                        const bodyAsJson = JSON.parse(message.body) as T;
                        observer.next(bodyAsJson);
                    });
                    }, error => {
                    // 👇propagate the error
                    observer.error(error);
                });

                // 👇handle the unsubscription
                return () => {
                    if (subscription) {
                    subscription.unsubscribe();
                    }
                    connection.close();
                };
            });
        }



4️⃣ Yay! Now let’s use our new service in RaceService. Inject the WsService in RaceService and replace
    the code in the live method to use our brand new connect method with the channel /race/${raceId}.
    🧠We want to stop listening to the live race when its status is finished, 🔥so we’re going to
    chain a call to the takeWhile operator. As we want to include the last message, where the status
    will be FINISHED, we can use the inclusive parameter of the takeWhile operator:

        live(raceId: number): Observable<LiveRaceModel> {
            return this.wsService
            .connect<LiveRaceModel>(`/race/${raceId}`)
            .pipe(takeWhile(liveRace => liveRace.status !== 'FINISHED', /* include last value */ true));
        }

    Relaunch your application: everything should work!



5️⃣ ⚠️⚠️But the WsService is sadly not very testable: it uses WebSocket and Webstomp directly, so we
    can’t mock these in our tests…​Like with our UserService we did not want to make real HTTP requests
    in tests, here we don’t want to establish a real WebSocket connection for our unit tests.

    🧠Let’s refactor our WsService a bit to make it testable and we’re done.

    🔥🔥That’s a great time to play with an advanced feature of the dependency injection system!
    The goal is to inject WebSocket and Webstomp in WsService rather than using them directly. Once
    they are injected, a unit test can easily replace them with fake implementations
    (read the chapter 14 on Dependency Injection if that’s not clear).

    ✅ First we need to create two tokens for the two dependencies, and map them to the real WebSocket
    and Webstomp symbols. To do so, create a new file app.tokens.ts and add the two following
    InjectionToken, which is the recommended way to create an injection token
    (it makes sure that the tokens are unique).
    app.tokens.ts:

        import { InjectionToken, Type } from '@angular/core';
        import * as WebstompClient from 'webstomp-client';

        export const WEBSOCKET = new InjectionToken<Type<WebSocket>>('WebSocket', 
            { providedIn: 'root', factory: () => WebSocket });
        export const WEBSTOMP = new InjectionToken<typeof WebstompClient>('WebstompClient', 
            { providedIn: 'root', factory: () => WebstompClient });
    


6️⃣ With these providers, it is now possible to inject the dependencies in WsService. To do so, use
    the following injection:

        private readonly WebSocket: Type<WebSocket> = inject(WEBSOCKET);
        private readonly Webstomp: typeof WebstompClient = inject(WEBSTOMP);
    
    As you can see we are now injecting two private fields WebSocket and Webstomp using inject().
    🔥The first field we inject is the type WebSocket, but the second one is, sadly, not easy to type,
    so we’ll go with typeof WebstompClient.

    ✅Use these fields in the connect method (you just have to add this. in front of WebSocket and
    Webstomp 😉).

        connect<T>(channel: string): Observable<T> {
            return new Observable((observer: Subscriber<T>) => {
                // 👇create the WebSocket connection
                const connection: WebSocket = new this.WebSocket(`${environment.wsBaseUrl}/ws`);

                // 👇create the stomp client with Webstomp
                const stompClient: WebstompClient.Client = this.Webstomp.over(connection);

                // 👇connect the stomp client
                let subscription: WebstompClient.Subscription;
                stompClient.connect({ login: '', passcode: '' }, () => {
                    // 👇subscribe to the specific channel
                    subscription = stompClient.subscribe(channel, message => {
                        // 👇emit the message received, after extracting the JSON from the body
                        const bodyAsJson = JSON.parse(message.body) as T;
                        observer.next(bodyAsJson);
                    });
                    }, error => {
                    // 👇propagate the error
                    observer.error(error);
                });

                // 👇handle the unsubscription
                return () => {
                    if (subscription) {
                    subscription.unsubscribe();
                    }
                    connection.close();
                };
            });
        }
    
    ✅You can now add the unit tests and have a look at how they are built to test the WsService!



7️⃣ Do not forget to copy all the test files and run ng test to be sure that everything is OK. 